	(text_box "DRV8212P H-bridge, inputs straight from TIM1. VM = VSYS. Logic VCC is +3V3_DRV: the MCU's +3V3 through Rh2, a fitted 0R. Swap Rh2 for a ferrite (C108301, 600R at 100 MHz) if driver noise ever shows on the MCU rail, which is also the ADC reference.\n\n- Ch1-Ch5 return to GND, NOT PGND. This is on purpose. The shunt sits between PGND and GND, and at PWM timescales the motor current loops cap -> bridge -> motor -> PGND -> back to the cap. If the caps land on the PGND side, that loop closes without ever crossing the shunt, and the ADC only sees the average draw instead of the real chopped current. Don't 'fix' this in layout.\n- Rh1 keeps DRV_EN low whenever nothing drives it, so any reset = bridge off. It pulls to PGND, the driver's own logic ground.\n- The driver's own OCP/TSD is the first layer of the protection stack. The rest is in the MCU sheet notes.\n\nLayout (6L, via-in-pad):\n- L2 is one unbroken GND plane. No PGND pour on inner layers, and no splits under the driver. Return currents image right under their traces.\n- Keep the PGND island tiny and top-layer only: driver GND pin and thermal pad, Rh1, NT1, and the top of the shunt. Nothing else lives there.\n- The shunt is the ONLY PGND-GND connection. No stitching vias, no pour merge. A second path splits the return current and the shunt under-reads.\n- Ch1-Ch5 hug the VM pin (100nF closest) and their ground pads drop straight into L2 with via-in-pad. The plane is only ~0.1mm down, so the switching loop (VM -> cap -> L2 -> shunt -> driver GND pin) closes right under the parts. Cluster the caps, shunt and driver anyway, plane current still spreads with distance.\n- Kelvin taps (ISNS_K+/-) come off the shunt pads themselves, routed as a tight pair over solid GND, away from the power loop. No power current on the sense traces, ever."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 209.55 27.94 0)
		(size 72.39 132.7149)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
